{Page clustering}\index{page clustering} provides two distinct
operational benefits depending on this factor:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{{Sparse Page Size Spectra}
	    \index{page size spectrum!sparse} (Large $W$):}] On
	    architectures with a wide gap between base page sizes and
	    the first non-trivial superpage size, page clustering acts
	    as a bridge. However, this is treated as a trivial case: it
	    merely shaves an additive constant off the required order of
	    superpage allocations without changing underlying memory
	    management algorithms. Because there tends to be exponential
	    to double exponential drop-off in likelihood of availability
	    with order, and at the same time the latency and internal
	    fragmentation issues rapidly become progressively more
	    aggravated once beyond some threshold typically
	    significantly smaller than the first nontrivial superpage
	    size. Decent odds can be achieved only really for orders 1,
	    2 and to the chagrin of SPARC\index{SPARC} and Alpha
	    \index{Alpha} partisans, maybe only sometimes 3. Hence a
	    plausible $Z$ might be:
\begin{eqnarray}
Z & \approx & 2^{-\Omega} g_1
\\
	& \approx & K\cdot 2^{(W-\Omega)_+}
\\
B & \approx  & 2^{(W-\Omega)_+}
\\
\mathop{\mathrm{lg}}B = \beta & \approx & (W-\Omega)_+
\end{eqnarray}
	    where $\Omega$ can be empirically tuned and $\Omega = 2$ or
	    $\Omega = 3$ are likely to balance success allocating
	    superpages of the first nontrivial size $g_1$ against how
	    intensive of internal fragmentation and latency mitigation
	    would be required to satisfactorily perform with such a $Z$. \\
	    However, the larger the $Z$, the more aggressive of
	    mitigation measures for latency and internal fragmentation
	    are needed. Initial provisional empirical results suggest
	    ca. 256~KiB is tolerable without latency and internal
	    fragmentation mitigation, albeit only for the sake of user
	    interaction. Linux porting efforts also found 256~KiB
	    required relatively minimal effort to retrofit, where
	    exceeding $\texttt{PAGE\_SIZE} > \texttt{PMD\_SIZE}$
	    appeared to be the qualitative difficulty threshhold,
	    that is, where pagetable structure is truncated.

    \item[\textbf{{Dense Spectra}\index{page size spectrum!dense} and
	    Allocation Guarantees:}] Where {page clustering}
	    \index{page clustering} achieves a profound, qualitative
	    breakthrough is for smaller superpage\index{superpaging}
	    sizes. By maintaining physical allocations at the $Z$
	    boundary, Telix\index{Telix} can \textit{guarantee that
	    allocations for smaller-size superpages will never fail
	    due to external fragmentation}. This eliminates the need for
	    reserved pool strategies for those smaller superpage sizes
	    like those of {HP-UX}\index{HP-UX} and reduces the need for
	    heavyweight, unpredictable runtime memory compaction as
	    sometimes has afflicted Linux\index{Linux}.

    \item[\textbf{Small Minimum Mapping Granularities} $K$:]
	    There are few lower bound constraints on $K$. In principle,
	    user PTEs need not even be smaller than the memory mapped,
	    and pagetable space conservation techniques can enable at
	    least one user process to run. Page clustering itself
	    doesn't demand space for the sub-$Z$ mappings apart from the
	    PTEs and certainly not on any sort of physical accounting
	    basis, rendering VAX-like\index{VAX} scenarios eminently
	    feasible to support without conflicts with advanced features.
	    The 1~KiB {MIPS\index{MIPS} \texttt{PageGrain} extension}
	    \index{PageGrain@\texttt{PageGrain} extension (MIPS)} is
	    the most plausible hardware support vehicle for
	    demonstrating the range of mapping granularities
	    supportable.
\end{description}

\section{Pervasive Extent-Based Accounting and Coremaplessness}
\label{sec:mem_coremapless}

Sublinear physical memory management metadata is achieved in Telix not
through clustering, but through \textit{Pervasive Extent-Based Internal
Accounting} and true \textit{Coremaplessness}.

Traditional operating systems reserve a contiguous array of physical
page descriptors at boot time to track frame metadata. This imposes a
severe penalty in the context of Wulf and McKee's ``Memory Wall''
\footcite{wulf95memorywall}. In modern execution environments, CPU
speeds vastly outpace memory access times, making cache sizes the true
bottleneck. The global metadata arrays of legacy kernels consume
critical cache space, putting kernel bookkeeping in direct competition
with user workloads for L2 and L3 cache residency. TLBs typically have
special-case kernel optimisations or otherwise have superpage sizes
large enough to direct map physical memory into kernelspace with very
few TLB entries, so the ``wall'' isn't a factor with the TLB.

Telix intends to achieve a coremapless architecture by utilizing
physical addresses—specifically Page Frame Numbers (PFNs) quantized by
$Z$—as the direct, raw handles for allocation and deallocation
operations.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dynamic Subsystem Metadata:} Rather than preallocating
	    metadata globally for every frame, any required metadata is
	    allocated dynamically strictly within the specific
	    subsystems (e.g., the page cache) that need it.
    \item \textbf{Extent-Based Indexing:} When frames are tracked within
	    these subsystem index structures, they are not indexed as
	    singular pages. Pervasive extent-based accounting dictates
	    that groups of them are tracked as power-of-two sized,
	    power-of-two aligned memory ranges.
\end{itemize}

To manage the raw physical frames underneath this accounting layer,
Telix employs a lock-free adaptation of the \textit{LLFree} allocator
\footcite{litz2021llfree}. The choice of LLFree is deliberate, yet
incidental to coremaplessness itself: LLFree is utilized primarily
because it avoids the cache misses and heavy lock contention associated
with traditional buddy allocators (such as Linux's, which manipulates
linked lists embedded inside the global page descriptor array). By
relying on in-band metadata within free frames, LLFree further preserves
the coremapless cache profile.

\section{Virtual Metadata, Hardware Walkers, and Inverted Page Tables}
\label{sec:mem_virtual_metadata}

Addressing virtual memory metadata overhead presents a distinct set of
trade-offs, dictated heavily by CPU architecture design choices.

\subsection{Mitigating Hardware Page-Table Walkers}

A major challenge in modern CPU design is the presence of mandatory
hardware page-table walkers. On architectures that enforce multi-level
hierarchical page tables (e.g., x86-64, ARM64, RISC-V), hardware walkers
render pure Inverted Page Tables (IPT) unusable for direct hardware
MMU fills.

To mitigate virtual metadata bloat under mandatory hardware walkers,
Telix proposes \textit{Extent-Driven Page Table Sharing}:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Canonical address space mappings are maintained
		independently of the hardware page tables in highly
		compressed software structures, combining
		\textit{Adaptive Radix Trees (ART)} (for dense extents)
		and \textit{B+ Trees} (for sparse ranges).
    \item While these software structures were initially conceived for
	    pure software-refill architectures, they provide a powerful
		coordination layer for mitigating hardware walkers. By
		using the ART/B+ trees to canonicalize large memory
		ranges, the OS can easily identify massive contiguous
		shared regions.
    \item Telix leverages this software extent knowledge to safely link
	    or clone the actual underlying hardware page table sub-trees
		across multiple processes. This allows thousands of
		processes to share a shared library or IPC ring while
		sharing the physical PTE structures, drastically
		reducing virtual memory footprints.
    \item To avoid cache-line contention and lock bottlenecks on
	    multi-core systems, shared page table nodes will employ
		localized replication mechanisms.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Inverted Page Tables and Software-Refill Architectures}

On CPU architectures featuring software-managed TLB refills (e.g., MIPS,
software-refill RISC-V profiles) or native hardware IPT support (e.g.,
64-bit SPARC, IA-64 Virtual Hash Page Tables [VHPT], and IBM POWER
Hashed Page Tables [HPT] and Segment Lookaside Buffers [SLB]), Telix
will actively utilize Inverted Page Tables.

In these environments, the IPT contains one entry per physical frame,
indexed via a hash of the Virtual Address and Address Space ID (ASID).
Telix will explore whether hardware-unassisted IPTs in pure
software-refill architectures provide a net performance benefit over
direct ART/B+ extent tree walks, treating software-refill IPT
optimisations as a primary experimental benchmark for the project.

\section{Low-Latency Optimization: Incremental Faulting and Tail Packing}
\label{sec:mem_latency_opt}

To minimize I/O and fault latency, the proposed memory architecture
incorporates two key structural mechanisms:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Incremental Anonymous Memory Faulting:}] For
	    Copy-On-Write (COW) and Zero-Fill-On-Demand (ZFOD) anonymous
		memory, Telix will implement incremental fault handling.
		Rather than allocating and zeroing full allocation
		blocks ($Z$) on initial access, the kernel incrementally
		faults sub-fragments ($K$), reducing initial fault
		stalls and optimizing bitblitting latency for
		interactive applications.

    \item[\textbf{In-Memory Tail Packing:}] Small files and trailing
	    file fragments in the page cache frequently waste memory due
	    to internal alignment padding. Telix plans to support
	    in-memory tail packing, packing sub-page file tails into
	    shared physical frames to maximize cache efficiency and
	    reduce storage bus pressure.
\end{description}

\section{Hardware Capability Bounds: CHERI and Morello}
\label{sec:mem_cheri}

The extent-based accounting used throughout the VM subsystem aligns
naturally with hardware-enforced capability architectures, specifically
\textit{CHERI} and ARM Morello.

When compiled for capability hardware, Telix's software Memory Morsels
(base, offset, length) will materialize directly as 128-bit hardware fat
pointers. This transfers spatial memory safety enforcement from software
checks to the CPU hardware pipeline, providing unforgeable,
hardware-bounded memory protection for shared-memory I/O rings and
Framekernel capability domains.

