\section{Framekernels, Multikernels and Address Spaces}

Asterinas'\index{Asterinas} framekernel\index{framekernel}
\footcite{peng24} method shares a kernel address space within the
lowest-level domains it covers. Barrelfish's\index{Barrelfish}
multikernel\index{multikernel}\footcite{baum09} in turn uses separate
kernel address spaces for the domains it manages. Telix is intended to
leverage the multikernel\index{multikernel} tactic to ``softly
quasi-partition'' SSI (Single System Image)
\index{SSI (Single System Image)} nodes of a cluster\index{cluster} to
domains within which scalability\index{scalability} is feasible or
otherwise more manageable. Within the ``soft quasi-partitions'', the
framekernel\index{framekernel} strategy is then used to reduce the
overheads microkernels\index{microkernel} might potentially suffer from
{address space}\index{address space} switching and forms of IPC
\index{IPC} demanding such.
